WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.240
Is our encryption in transit
as strong as we think?

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.190
Cipher suites decide what gets encrypted,
and how strongly.

00:00:07.565 --> 00:00:10.455
Wrong choices leave
data readable by design.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.160
First, weak settings.

00:00:13.605 --> 00:00:19.195
We agreed on TLS 1.3, but one
endpoint still allows a legacy cipher.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.070
Legacy settings stay on for compatibility.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:24.225
They are invisible in logs.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:28.520
Consider modern ciphers only,
and phase out the legacy ones.

00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:30.893
Second, a downgrade.

00:00:31.238 --> 00:00:34.268
Both sides support TLS 1.3,

00:00:34.418 --> 00:00:38.448
but a custom client fell back to TLS 1.0.

00:00:38.718 --> 00:00:40.728
Its own retry logic did it.

00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:44.543
The negotiation is silent,
and it forces a weak protocol.

00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:46.738
Consider failing on a weak version,

00:00:47.108 --> 00:00:50.278
with no fallback below TLS 1.2.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:52.540
Third, the key exchange.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.750
Session keys are protected by
the server's static private key.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.300
Without forward secrecy,

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.840
whoever gets that key later
can decrypt old traffic.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.460
That is harvest now, decrypt later.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.770
Consider a key exchange with
fresh keys each session,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.640
and hybrid post-quantum where available.

00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:12.893
What good looks like.

00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:15.288
Hardening is not a single checklist.

00:01:15.723 --> 00:01:17.673
Old versions are often a default,

00:01:18.143 --> 00:01:20.713
and configurations
drift when teams change.

00:01:21.068 --> 00:01:22.178
Continuous scanning,

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:24.298
an owner for every red finding,

00:01:24.708 --> 00:01:29.538
and a plan for hybrid post-quantum key
exchange usually keep it contained.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:32.640
A suggested first step.

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:35.935
Ask the CIO or the CISO for four numbers.

00:01:36.315 --> 00:01:41.225
Systems offering only TLS 1.2 or newer,
with modern suites.

00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:46.165
Systems still accepting TLS 1.0 or 1.1.

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:48.435
Key exchanges with forward secrecy,

00:01:48.885 --> 00:01:51.695
and connections using hybrid post-quantum.

00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:55.267
Encryption settings drift over time.

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:58.792
Scan continuously,
and give every finding an owner.

